#!/usr/bin/env perl

package Trog::Skill::ScratchConfig;

#ABSTRACT: A throwaway configuration directory, with a throwaway secret store to match.

use 5.041;

use strict;
use warnings FATAL => 'all';
use re '/aasx';

use FindBin::libs;

use Crypt::PRNG();
use File::Copy qw{copy};
use File::Path qw{make_path remove_tree};
use File::Slurper();
use File::Slurper::Temp();
use File::Temp();
use Getopt::Long qw{GetOptionsFromArray};
use Pod::Usage   qw{pod2usage};
use YAML::XS();

use Trog::Config();
use Trog::Hypervisors::Config();
use Provisioner::Cookbook();
use Trog::Secrets();
use Provisioner::Utils();

=head1 NAME

scratch_config - a throwaway configuration directory, with a throwaway secret store to match

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    eval "$(.claude/skills/provisioning-recipes/scripts/scratch_config)"
    # TROG_PROVISIONER_CONFIG and TROG_SCRATCH_PASS are now set

    scratch_config --print-dir       # just the path

    # Keep a hypervisor that needs a credential, with a temporary token on stdin
    printf '%s\n' "$TEMPORARY_TOKEN" | scratch_config --keep NAME
    scratch_config --remove DIR      # throw one away

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Builds a configuration directory that can be provisioned against without going
anywhere near the real one, and prints shell to point at it.

What it copies, and what it deliberately does not:

=over 4

=item The settings of the installation

Taken out of the C<_global> of C<_base> in the installation's F<recipes.yaml>,
key for key, for every key that L<Provisioner::Cookbook/global_schema>
declares.  That is who administers a guest, how it reaches the network, and the
address pool it draws from: a guest built here gets an address out of the real
pool, so it cannot be handed one that is already spoken for.  The assignment is
written to the scratch F<ips.db>, so the installation does not grow a record for
a guest that will not exist by lunchtime.

The recipes of that C<_base> are left behind, which is the next item.

=item F<admin_authorized_keys>

Copied when the installation has one.  These are public keys rather than a
credential, so this is the settings case and not the F<secrets.kdbx> one.

A throwaway pair is minted when it has none, so that a scratch run works on an
installation nobody has seeded yet -- C<bin/new_config> refuses to build a guest
with nobody authorized on it.  Nothing is lost by the stand-in: what actually
reaches a scratch guest is the domain key, which
L<Provisioner::Recipe::ubuntu> authorizes on every guest it builds whatever else
is in the list.

=item F<recipes.yaml>

B<Not> copied.  Built here, holding a C<_base> and nothing else: the settings
above, a C<data_source> inside this directory, and the installation's C<mirror>
when it has one.

Copying it was the bug this replaced.  The installation's C<_base> names
credentials out of a KeePass store this harness cannot open -- it builds a
throwaway one precisely because it cannot -- so every scratch guest inherited a
registrar whose credentials were made up, and inherited the whole fleet recipe
set besides, which is how a guest asked for two recipes came to install a
certificate authority and a DNS server it never named.

A scratch guest now gets what C<new_guest> named and the C<data> recipe.  The
mirror is the one thing carried over: it is infrastructure rather than something
a recipe under test has an opinion about, and without it every build fetches its
packages from the far side of the internet.

=item F<hypervisors.conf>

Copied when there is one.  Without it there is no fleet, and a throwaway guest
gets built on whatever libvirt this machine has rather than on the hypervisor
the real ones live on -- which on a workstation means it does not get built at
all.

=item F<recipes.d/>

B<Not> copied, deliberately.  new_config gathers secrets across every domain it
can see, so copying these would mean needing every password in the real store
in order to build one throwaway guest.  Empty, and with the C<_base> built rather
than copied, there is normally no secret to want at all.

=item F<secrets.kdbx>

Not copied.  A new one is built holding a made-up value for every C<secret:>
reference in the scratch F<recipes.yaml> -- normally none, what is built there
naming no credential -- under a password printed to stdout.

This never opens the installation's store.  The password of the scratch store
is printed, so whatever is in it can end up in a transcript or a log.  A
production credential must never be there.

What F<hypervisors.conf> names is the exception to the made-up values, such as
the token of a Linode or SolusVM block.  A made-up token cannot reach a real
hypervisor.  So each block that names a secret is left out of the scratch
F<hypervisors.conf>, and the report names it.  A scratch guest then goes to a
hypervisor that needs no credential, and C<bin/destroy> asks only those.

C<--keep> keeps a block for a scratch guest that must go to such a hypervisor.
Its value is a temporary token that the operator made for this, with only the
access that a build needs, and that the operator revokes after the teardown.
It is read from standard input, one line for each reference that the kept
blocks name, in sorted order.

If every block that is not kept names a secret and nothing is kept, this dies,
because nothing would be left to build on.

=back

The values it makes up are visibly fake -- C<throwaway:group/entry/field>.
Anything that reaches a real service with one gets rejected, which is the
intent: a recipe that talks to a registrar will fail at the point it tries,
rather than succeeding against somebody's live DNS.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<--dir> DIR

Build it here rather than in a fresh temporary directory.

=item B<--print-dir>

Print the directory alone, no shell.

=item B<--cpan-tests>

Run the test suites of what the guest installs from CPAN, by writing
C<cpan_notest: 0> into the scratch C<_base>'s C<_global>.  Off by default, as it
is on a real guest; the skill says when a build wants it.

=item B<--cache> NAME

Provision through the fetch cache NAME, by writing C<cache: NAME> into the
scratch C<_base>'s C<_global>.  NAME is a cache built from this same scratch
configuration, which has an authority of its own; the skill says how.

=item B<--keep> NAME

Keep the block NAME of F<hypervisors.conf>, which names a secret, and read a
temporary token for each of its references from standard input.  Repeat it to
keep more than one.  See F<secrets.kdbx> above for what the token must be.

=item B<--remove> DIR

Delete a scratch directory.  Refuses anything that does not look like one, so a
mistyped variable cannot take out the real configuration.

=back

=cut

our $MARKER = '.trog-scratch-config';

sub main {
    my @args = @_;

    my ( $dir, $print_dir, $remove, $cpan_tests, $cache, @keep, $help );
    GetOptionsFromArray(
        \@args,
        'dir=s'       => \$dir,
        'keep=s'      => \@keep,
        'cpan-tests' => \$cpan_tests,
        'cache=s'    => \$cache,
        'print-dir'  => \$print_dir,
        'remove=s'   => \$remove,
        'help'       => \$help,
    );
    pod2usage( -exitval => 0, -verbose => 2, -input => __FILE__ ) if $help;

    return remove($remove) if $remove;

    my $source = Trog::Config->dir;
    $dir //= File::Temp::tempdir( 'trog-scratch-XXXXXXXX', TMPDIR => 1 );

    my ( $password, $has_fleet, $keys_copied, $real, $left_out, @refs ) = build(
        $source, $dir,
        cpan_tests     => $cpan_tests,
        cache          => $cache,
        keep           => \@keep,
        token_for      => \&read_token,
    );

    if ($print_dir) {
        print "$dir\n";
        return 0;
    }

    my $report = "Scratch configuration in $dir\n";
    $report .= "  copied from $source: the settings of _base" . ( $has_fleet   ? ", hypervisors.conf\n"                                 : "\n" );
    $report .= '  admin_authorized_keys ' .                     ( $keys_copied ? "copied, so a scratch guest lets the same people in\n" : "built here, the installation having none to copy\n" );
    $report .= "  recipes.yaml built here, so none of the installation's credentials are wanted\n";
    $report .= "  no hypervisors.conf there, so the hypervisor is this machine\n"
      unless $has_fleet;
    $report .= "  recipes.d/ left empty, so a guest gets what new_guest names and nothing else\n";
    $report .= "  cpan_notest: 0 in _base's _global, so CPAN test suites run\n"       if $cpan_tests;
    $report .= "  cache: $cache in _base's _global, so guests provision through it\n" if $cache;
    $report .= "  secrets.kdbx built with " . scalar(@refs) . " throwaway value" . ( @refs == 1 ? '' : 's' ) . ":\n";
    $report .= join( q{}, map { "    $_\n" } @refs );
    $report .= "  and the temporary token for each of these, from standard input.  Revoke each after the teardown:\n" . join( q{}, map { "    $_\n" } @$real )
      if @$real;
    $report .= "  left out of hypervisors.conf, because they name a credential: " . join( ', ', @$left_out ) . "\n"
      if @$left_out;
    $report .= "\n";

    # STDERR because the caller runs this as eval "$(scratch_config)": whatever
    # reaches STDOUT is executed by their shell, so the two export lines below
    # are the only things allowed there.
    print {*STDERR} $report;    ## no critic (ProhibitPrintSTDERR)

    print "export TROG_PROVISIONER_CONFIG=$dir\n" . "export TROG_SCRATCH_PASS=$password\n";
    return 0;
}

sub build {
    my ( $source, $dir, %opts ) = @_;

    make_path("$dir/recipes.d");
    make_path("$dir/data");

    # What says this is ours to delete later.
    File::Slurper::Temp::write_text( "$dir/$MARKER", "Built by scratch_config; safe to remove.\n" );

    # Somebody has to be authorized or bin/new_config will not build a guest at
    # all.  Copied when the installation has keys -- they are public, so this is
    # the settings case rather than the secrets one -- and a throwaway pair minted
    # when it has none, so a scratch run works before anybody has seeded one.
    # Nothing is lost by the stand-in: what reaches a scratch guest is the domain
    # key, which Provisioner::Recipe::ubuntu authorizes on every guest it builds
    # whatever else is in the list.
    my $ED25519_BITS = 256;
    my $keys_copied  = -f "$source/admin_authorized_keys" ? 1 : 0;    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f) -- whether there is one to copy is the whole question

    if ($keys_copied) {
        copy( "$source/admin_authorized_keys", "$dir/admin_authorized_keys" )
          or die "Could not copy $source/admin_authorized_keys into the scratch config: $!\n";
    }
    else {
        Provisioner::Utils::write_ssh_keypair( "$dir/scratch_admin_key", Ed25519 => $ED25519_BITS, 'scratch' );

        # It sets no mode of its own, so the umask decides and the private half
        # lands readable by anyone on this machine.  Setting it is the caller's,
        # the same way Provisioner::Recipe::ubuntu's guest_keypair does it.
        chmod 0600, "$dir/scratch_admin_key", "$dir/scratch_admin_key.pub";

        copy( "$dir/scratch_admin_key.pub", "$dir/admin_authorized_keys" )
          or die "Could not write a throwaway key into the scratch config: $!\n";
    }

    # And the _base, built rather than taken: see the POD above for what copying
    # it cost.  The settings come across because a guest cannot be built without
    # them; the recipes beside them do not.  The data source is inside this
    # directory, so a throwaway guest reads and writes nothing of the
    # installation's.
    my %global = ( source_globals($source), data_source => "$dir/data" );

    # CPAN test suites, when what the build is testing is what gets installed.
    # Otherwise skipped, as on a real guest: see the skill, and
    # Provisioner::Recipe::perl's cpan_notest, which _global reaches.
    #
    # And a fetch cache, when the build is to go through one: see
    # Provisioner::Recipe::fetchcache.
    $global{cpan_notest} = 0            if $opts{cpan_tests};
    $global{cache}       = $opts{cache} if $opts{cache};

    File::Slurper::Temp::write_binary( "$dir/recipes.yaml", YAML::XS::Dump( { _base => { _global => \%global } } ) );

    # The fleet, when there is one.  Its absence is a legitimate state -- it
    # means this machine is the hypervisor -- so this is not fatal, but getting
    # it wrong silently sends the guest to the wrong libvirt.
    my $fleet     = 'hypervisors.conf';
    my $has_fleet = copy( "$source/$fleet", "$dir/$fleet" ) ? 1 : 0;

    my %needed = Trog::Secrets->needed( YAML::XS::Load( File::Slurper::read_binary("$dir/recipes.yaml") ) );
    my %unique = map { $_ => 1 } values %needed;
    my @refs   = sort keys %unique;

    # A block that names a secret goes, unless it is kept, and then its secret
    # is the temporary token that the operator gives.  The installation's store
    # is never opened: see secrets.kdbx in the POD.
    my $config = Trog::Hypervisors::Config->load( $has_fleet ? "$dir/$fleet" : undef );
    my %named  = map { $_ => 1 } $config->names;
    my @kept   = @{ $opts{keep} // [] };
    foreach my $name (@kept) {
        die "There is no hypervisor called $name in $source/$fleet to keep.\n" unless $named{$name};
    }

    my %keep     = map { $_ => 1 } @kept;
    my @left_out = grep { !$keep{$_} } $config->secret_blocks;
    die "Every hypervisor in $source/$fleet names a credential, so nothing is left to build a scratch guest on.  Keep one with --keep and a temporary token.\n"
      if %named && @left_out == keys %named;
    if (@left_out) {
        my %drop = map { $_ => 1 } @left_out;
        File::Slurper::Temp::write_text( "$dir/$fleet", without_blocks( File::Slurper::read_text("$dir/$fleet"), %drop ) );
    }

    my @fleet_refs = @kept ? $config->secret_references(@kept) : ();
    my %real       = map { $_ => $opts{token_for}->($_) } @fleet_refs;

    my $password = Crypt::PRNG::random_string(20);

    # Visibly fake, so that anything reaching a real service with one is refused
    # rather than quietly working.  What the values are is this harness's
    # business; making the database is Trog::Secrets'.
    Trog::Secrets->create(
        "$dir/secrets.kdbx", $password,
        ( map { $_ => 'throwaway:' . substr( $_, length 'secret:' ) } @refs ),
        %real,
    );
    chmod 0600, "$dir/secrets.kdbx";

    return ( $password, $has_fleet, $keys_copied, \@fleet_refs, \@left_out, @refs );
}

# A temporary token from standard input, one line for each reference.
sub read_token {
    my ($ref) = @_;

    my $token = readline STDIN;
    die "No temporary token on standard input for $ref.\n" unless defined $token;
    chomp $token;
    die "The temporary token for $ref is empty.\n" unless length $token;
    return $token;
}

# The text of a fleet without the blocks in %drop.  A block runs from its
# header to the next header.
sub without_blocks {
    my ( $text, %drop ) = @_;

    my $keep = 1;
    my @kept;
    foreach my $line ( split m/^/m, $text ) {
        $keep = !$drop{$1} if $line =~ m/\A\s*\[([^\]]+)\]/;
        push @kept, $line if $keep;
    }
    return join q{}, @kept;
}

# What the installation configures its guests with, and its mirror.  Read key by
# key rather than the file copied: everything else in its _base is either a
# credential this harness cannot hold or a recipe the guest under test did not
# ask for.
sub source_globals {
    my ($source) = @_;

    my $file = "$source/recipes.yaml";
    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f)
    return () unless -f $file;

    my $conf = eval { YAML::XS::Load( File::Slurper::read_binary($file) ) };
    return () unless ref $conf eq 'HASH';

    my $said   = $conf->{_base}{_global} // {};
    my %schema = Provisioner::Cookbook->global_schema;

    # Not libdir: a vendor's recipes are theirs, and can want credentials that
    # this harness cannot hold.
    return map { $_ => $said->{$_} } grep { defined $said->{$_} && $_ ne 'libdir' } ( keys %{ $schema{properties} }, 'mirror' );
}

sub remove {
    my ($dir) = @_;

    die "Refusing to remove '$dir': no $MARKER in it, so it is not one of ours.\n"
      unless -f "$dir/$MARKER";    ## no critic (ValuesAndExpressions::ProhibitFiletest_f)

    remove_tree($dir);
    print {*STDERR} "Removed $dir\n";    ## no critic (ProhibitPrintSTDERR) -- as above: STDOUT is the caller's shell input
    return 0;
}

exit main(@ARGV) unless caller;

1;
